{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ITEM 2 VERIFICATION — `--yes` IS accepted on BOTH link subcommands. Verified directly against\nthe subcommands themselves (not inferred from the `workitem delete` sibling), per the 25ae\nlesson that a sibling's flag support proves nothing.\n\nLocal binary is `acli version 1.3.19-stable` — the EXACT version the fleet caches\n(`acli-Linux-1.3.19-stable` in the bridge workflow), so this is same-version evidence, not a\nnewer-build guess.\n\n    $ acli jira workitem link create --help\n          --yes                Confirm link creation without prompting\n\n    $ acli jira workitem link delete --help\n          --yes                Confirm link deletion without prompting\n\nBoth subcommands prompt for confirmation by default and both accept `--yes` to skip it. With\n`stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL` (acli_subprocess.py) that prompt reads EOF and the Go CLI reports\n`X Error: command cancelled` — which is exactly the observed string, and exactly the mechanism\nbug 3256/cfd6 already documented for `workitem delete`.\n\nNote the asymmetry that misled the original ticket: `--json` is genuinely rejected on these two\nsubcommands (story 25ae, correct), but `--yes` is genuinely accepted. The two flags had to be\nchecked separately; conflating them is what produced the \"verify before adding\" caution.\n\nRoot cause is therefore CONFIRMED, not merely suspected: the missing `--yes` causes the\ncancellation, and the no-print `except CalledProcessError: _links_applied += 1` arm in\ndispatch_one.py converts that cancellation into a reported success.\n\nProceeding with both approved items."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786525851097811001,"uuid":"6151f8a9-7964-4df1-ae3a-d009b66e77f9"}